ASPIRATIONAL RED. Today this prints:

    EVENT DENIED: [I cannot delete files. My vocabulary only includes open]

expected.txt asserts PARSE ERROR, which is what it should print.

The interpreter's flow_parser takes everything up to the first `(` as the event
name, so an English sentence containing parentheses parses as a well-formed
invocation whose name happens to contain spaces and a full stop. Nothing
downstream rejects it on those grounds; the scope lookup rejects it because no
event is called that, and the caller is told `event-denied`.

WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS, and why it is pinned rather than shrugged at:
the two branches mean opposite things and route to opposite fixes.
`event-denied` says "that verb is real but you may not use it here" — the
permission model working. `parse-error` says "that was not Koru". Handing the
first to a caller who did the second misdiagnoses the failure.

It was found by an app doing exactly this for real. koru-examples
kopium-headless/live.k drives this interpreter from a live LLM and feeds every
dispatch outcome back to the model as a message. Asked to delete a file, the
model correctly declined IN PROSE, and the agent was told its own English was
"not in this agent's scope" — so the loop teaches the model the wrong lesson
about what went wrong. An interpreter whose source comes from a language model
gets prose as input routinely; this is the common case, not the edge.

A related observation worth keeping with this one: a cooperative model never
produces a genuine `event-denied`. Five out-of-vocabulary requests were probed
against claude-haiku-4.5 and it declined all five in prose rather than
inventing an event name. The one live `event-denied` on record is THIS bug.
The vocabulary wall's real customer is prompt/register drift — measured
separately: a prompt claiming a fourth verb the register block does not declare
gets `sync(handle: "note_0")` emitted immediately.

Fix direction, unruled: an event name should have to lex as an event name
(identifier segments, `.` and `-` between them, no spaces). That check belongs
in flow_parser, before the scope lookup ever runs.
